# Technician Scope Doctrine

Full doc: `docs/operations/technician-scope-doctrine-v1.md`  
Discovery audit: `docs/operations/technician-discovery-audit.md`  
Next contract: `docs/operations/repair-order-discovery-contract.md` (draft — observe before implement)

## Mental model

**Technician ≠ low-permission advisor.** Technician = **specialized production role**.

Wrong approach: hide buttons, leave surfaces available, read-only advisor UI.

Right approach: technicians **never enter** most surfaces. **Assigned work only.** Earn visibility through evidence.

## Default answer

When proposing technician access or nav:

> **No — unless it directly supports assigned work** (diagnose, perform, document).

**Burden of proof is on the surface seeking technician visibility.** Do not expand for convenience.

## Technicians own

Assigned ROs, inspection/diagnostic observations, work performed, labor completion, parts visibility for assigned work, vehicle history for assigned work, internal production notes.

## Technicians do not own

Customers, comms queues/recovery, follow-ups, decision pressure, approvals, scheduling pressure, shop-wide queues, financials, estimates, global customer/vehicle/RO search.

## Comms

Consume context **on assigned work only**. No queues, no recovery, no relationship workflows as primary destinations.

## Nav target (observation before build)

My Work → Assigned Repair Orders → Inspection → ARKademy — not Work, Communications, Customers, Vehicles, shop pressure.

## Current gaps (do not guess-close)

Shop-wide workboard and global RO index may still exceed v1 scope. Narrow only after floor observation — see doc § Current enforcement vs doctrine.

## Sequence

Doctrine → Authority → **Observation** → Workflow → Projection. Same as Inspection. Do not skip observation.
